    color: #FFFFFF; /* Changing text to white for better contrast */
}

.gdpr-options-btn {
    background-color: #FF9800; /* Orange */
    color: #FFFFFF; /* Changing text to white for better contrast */
}
```

### Additional Notes:
- Always verify contrast ratios using tools like the WebAIM Contrast Checker.
- Consider the size of your text. Larger text sizes allow for lower contrast ratios (3:1) but ensuring a 4.5:1 ratio or greater makes your content accessible to a wider audience.
- Keep in mind that CSS specifics such as `font-size` and `font-weight` influence whether text is considered 'normal' or 'large'. For WCAG, "large text" is defined as 18pt or 14pt bold (or roughly 24px and 18.66px), so adjust accordingly.
- Test your website with users or accessibility testing tools to ensure that these changes effectively improve your website's accessibility.

This guidance does not cover all potential accessibility or contrast issues but focuses on improving color contrast as highlighted in your request.

Yes, according to the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.1, content that is hidden using `aria-hidden="true"` should not contain focusable elements (such as `<a>` tags with `href` attributes, buttons, or form inputs) because it can lead to a confusing experience for screen reader users if interactive elements are announced as being present but visually hidden from sighted users.

Given the provided HTML source code, there are links (`<a>` tags) with the `target="_blank"` attribute that are marked as hidden with `aria-hidden="true"`, while also being focusable due to the presence of an `href` attribute. To remedy this and satisfy WCAG 2.1 criteria, you'd need to ensure that these elements are either not hidden from accessibility tools when they are focusable or made unfocusable when they are meant to be hidden.

One straightforward method to fix this issue is by removing the `aria-hidden="true"` attribute from the focusable elements. However, if those elements need to remain hidden for a specific reason (e.g., they are part of a carousel that only shows one slide at a time), another approach is to dynamically manage their focusability and visibility to assistive technologies, depending on their state (visible or hidden). This typically involves JavaScript to dynamically toggle `aria-hidden` and either set `tabindex="-1"` to make elements unfocusable or remove the `tabindex` attribute to allow focus.

Since modifying each link individually based on dynamic content might be impractical in this message, I'll provide a general idea of how you could approach this with JavaScript:

```javascript
// Example of JavaScript to dynamically manage focusable elements inside an area marked with aria-hidden="true"

function updateFocusableElementsVisibility(container) {
    // Find all elements within the container that are currently hidden from screen readers
    const hiddenElements = container.querySelectorAll('[aria-hidden="true"]');

    hiddenElements.forEach(hiddenEl => {
        // For each hidden element, find all focusable elements
        const focusableElements = hiddenEl.querySelectorAll('a[href], button, input, textarea, select, details, [tabindex]:not([tabindex="-1"])');

        focusableElements.forEach(el => {
            // Make those elements unfocusable
            el.setAttribute('tabindex', '-1');
            // Add additional handling as needed, such as hiding from screen readers,
            // but be careful with changing aria-hidden directly on focusable elements
        });
    });

    // Conversely, find previously hidden elements now being shown and restore their focusability
    // This step assumes you have a way to track these changes or mark these elements accordingly
    // This is just a conceptual guidance and should be adapted to your specific case
    const previouslyHiddenElementsNowVisible = container.querySelectorAll('.your-specific-selector');
    previouslyHiddenElementsNowVisible.forEach(el => {
        el.removeAttribute('tabidx');
        // Restore aria-hidden to false or remove it, depending on what makes sense for your content
    });
}
```

Please note, this script is quite generic and would need to be adapted to fit the specifics of your project, such as adjusting selectors and integrating the logic with the events or conditions that change the visibility state of the content.

By ensuring that focusable elements are only focusable when they are visible and appropriately marked for accessibility tools, you can improve the navigational experience for keyboard and screen reader users, complying with WCAG success criteria.

Certainly, to improve accessibility and ensure it meets the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.1 requirements, specifically the success criterion which mandates that every form element should have a label, we need to add a label element associated with the input field. A label can be associated with an input element by matching the `for` attribute of the label with the `id` of the input element. Here’s how to fix it:

```html
<label for="txtSearch" class="visually-hidden">Search:</label>
<input class="search-form_input" id="txtSearch" spellcheck="false" type="text" name="s" autocomplete="off" placeholder="">
```

In addition, I've added a class `visually-hidden` on the label. This class is often used to hide labels visually but still make them accessible to screen readers and other assistive technologies. This is useful in cases where the design doesn't incorporate visible labels. Here’s an example definition of the `visually-hidden` class, which ensures that the element is accessible to screen readers but not visible on the screen:

```css
.visually-hidden { 
    position: absolute; 
    width: 1px; 
    height: 1px; 
    margin: -1px; 
    border: 0; 
    padding: 0; 
    white-space: nowrap; 
    clip: rect(0, 0, 0, 0); 
    overflow: hidden; 
    clip-path: inset(50%);
}
```

Here, the CSS properties ensure the label is not visible on the screen but accessible by screen readers, completing the accessibility improvement for the input form field. Remember to add descriptive text within the `<label>` to describe the function of the input field, which aids users in understanding the purpose of the input field.

This approach is aligned with the WCAG 2.1 guidelines, ensuring better accessibility for users who rely on screen readers and other assistive technologies to navigate and interact with web content.

To address the accessibility issue related to ensuring that the contrast between foreground and background colors meets the WCAG 2 AA contrast ratio thresholds according to WCAG 2.1, specific CSS adjustments should be made. However, the provided source code does not include any color values for either the text or the background. Therefore, I will provide a general guidance on how to approach the issue:

1. **Identifying Color Values**: First, determine the current color values used for both the text (foreground) and the background against which the text is being rendered. This information might be available in an external CSS file or inline styles.

2. **Calculating Contrast Ratio**: Once you have identified the color values, use a contrast ratio calculator to determine the current contrast ratio. A simple online search will provide several free tools for this purpose.

3. **Adjusting Colors to Meet WCAG 2 AA**: WCAG 2.1 requires a contrast ratio of at least 4.5:1 for normal text and 3:1 for large text to meet AA standards. If the current contrast ratio does not satisfy these requirements, adjust the colors accordingly. Keep in mind that increasing the contrast helps those with visual impairments, including color blindness, to better distinguish text.

Here's a hypothetical CSS solution assuming you have a light grey background (`#E0E0E0`) and want the text to be fully accessible:

```css
/* Example CSS adjustments for enhanced contrast ratio */
a, span {
    /* Assuming the default text color is too light against a white background, we change it to a darker shade */
    color: #333333; /* Providing good contrast against a lighter background */
}

/* Further enhancements for links to ensure they stand out */
a.button, a.cookie-consent-settings, .sel-language {
    background-color: #005A9C; /* A darker blue for button backgrounds */
    color: #FFFFFF; /* White text to ensure high contrast */
    border: none; /* Assuming you might not need border, but it's up to design */
}

.footer-copy, .tooltip-lang {
    color: #666666; /* Slightly lighter but still with an acceptable contrast ratio */
}

/* Ensure that hover and focus states also meet contrast ratios */
a:hover, a:focus {
    color: #222222; /* Darker for better visibility */
    background-color: #CCCCCC; /* Lighter background when hovered/focused, if applicable */
}
```

These examples are simplistic and will need to be adjusted based on the actual design requirements and existing color scheme of the website. The key principle is to ensure that text and critical interactive elements stand out distinctly against their backgrounds. Using tools like the WebAIM Contrast Checker can help guide these adjustments to meet or exceed the recommended contrast ratios.

The code snippet presents several SVG icons, each with an `id` attribute defined. The WCAG 2.1 success criteria require that every `id` attribute value on a page is unique to prevent conflicts, particularly in JavaScript and CSS styling which might target those IDs. From the provided code, there's no direct repetition of `id` attributes within the given snippets. However, assuming that ensuring ID uniqueness within a broader context is the goal (e.g., across a website where these icons may be reused), we can approach fixing potential issues through a strategy of dynamically assigning or modifying `id` attributes when they are used multiple times.

Here's an example approach, assuming you're generating these icons dynamically on a webpage:

### Solution Concept

Whenever you insert an SVG (or any element with an ID) into your document dynamically, ensure you either:
- Modify its `id` dynamically to ensure it's unique.
- Remove the `id` if it's not needed for styling or scripting purposes.

### Example Fixes
These fixes presume the SVGs are statically defined in your HTML and you can directly edit the HTML. If these are generated dynamically, you'd apply similar logic in your JavaScript that handles the SVG element creation or insertion.

#### 1. Rename IDs Dynamically (Static HTML Example)

If you're directly editing the HTML, ensure each `id` is unique. Add a suffix or prefix based on the context. For static changes:

Before:
```html
<svg id="nav-tv-ico" ... ></svg>
<svg id="nav-plexamp-ico" ... ></svg>
<svg id="android" ... ></svg>
```

After (adding a unique suffix or changing IDs to be context-specific):
```html
<svg id="nav-tv-ico-page1" ... ></svg>
<svg id="nav-plexamp-ico-page1" ... ></svg>
<svg id="android-icon-footer" ... ></svg>
```

#### 2. Dynamically Remove or Adjust `id` Upon Insertion (JavaScript Example)

If your page inserts these SVGs dynamically via JavaScript and you don't need the IDs for styling or scripting, you could remove them or ensure uniqueness programmatically:

```javascript
// Example: Dynamically adjust or remove the ID
const svgs = document.querySelectorAll('svg');
svgs.forEach((svg, index) => {
    // If ID is not needed:
    svg.removeAttribute('id');

    // Or ensure uniqueness:
    const originalId = svg.getAttribute('id');
    if(originalandId) {
        svg.setAttribute('id', `${originalId}-${Date.now()}-${index}`);
    }
});
```

This JavaScript snippet iterates over all SVG elements, either removing their `id` attributes or appending a unique suffix based on the current timestamp and the element's index, ensuring the ID's uniqueness.

### Conclusion

Ensuring unique IDs is crucial for accessibility and overall functionality. The approach depends on whether you're working with static HTML content or dynamically generated content, adjusting accordingly.

To make the source code accessible according to the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.1, particularly addressing the criterion that ensures links have discernible text (useful for screen readers and other assistive technologies), you need to add text within the links that screen readers can announce. Since the current links only contain icons without textual content, here is how you can modify them:

```html
<a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://twitter.com/plex" data-pulldown="item-social-x"><i class="icomoon icon-social-x"></i><span class="visually-hidden">Twitter</span></a>, 
<a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://www.facebook.com/plexapp" data-pulldown="item-social-facebook"><i class="icomoon icon-social-facebook"></i><span class="visually-hidden">Facebook</span></a>, 
<a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://www.instagram.com/plex.tv/" data-pulldown="item-social-instagram"><i class="icomoon icon-social-instagram"></i><span class="visually-hidden">Instagram</span></a>, 
<a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://www.tiktok.com/@plex.tv" data-pulldown="item-social-tiktok"><i class="icomoon icon-social-tiktok"></i><span class="visually-hidden">TikTok</span></a>, 
<a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://www.linkedin.com/company/plex-inc" data-pulldown="item-social-linkedin"><i class="icomoon icon-social-linkedin"></i><span class="visually-hidden">LinkedIn</span></a>, 
<a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://www.youtube.com/user/plextvapp" data-pulldown="item-social-youtube"><i class="icomoon icon-social-youtube"></i><span class="visually-hidden">YouTube</span></a>
```

To ensure these textual descriptions are accessible for screen readers but not visible on the screen, you can use a class like `visually-hidden`. This class is commonly used in accessibility contexts to hide content visually but make it available to assist you, typically by moving the element off-screen. Here's an example CSS for the `visually-hidden` class; please adjust the values according to your project's CSS conventions:

```css
.visually-hidden {
    position: absolute !important;
    height: 1px; width: 1px; 
    overflow: hidden;
    clip: rect(1px, 1px, 1px, 1px);
    white-space: nowrap; /* added for security reasons in some screen readers */
}
```

Please note the use of `!important` to ensure these styles are applied over any other conflicting styles. This helps improve the accessibility of your web links by providing meaningful textual alternatives to the iconography.
